A simple bokeh server using FastAPI to return a HTML file containing and interactive time series plot.

To start the server:
- Create a virtual env using the requirements file
  ```
    python -m venv venv
    source venv/bin/activate
    pip install -r requirements.txt
  ```
- Run uvicorn server
  ```
    uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8765
  ```
  the reload flag triggers the server to restart whenever the code is edited, good if you are hacking around :)

To generate HTML file:
- Using cURL:
  ```
    curl -X 'POST' \
    'http://localhost:8765/plot' \
    -H 'accept: application/json' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -d '{
      "times": [
        "2025-06-10T00:00:00",
        "2025-06-10T01:00:00"
      ],
      "data": [
        "10", "12"
      ],
      "variable": "temperature",
      "units": "deg C"
      }' > plot.html
    ```
- Using docs page:
  Navigate to localhost:8765/docs
  Open `/plot` menu
  Press 'try it out'
  Paste the following to the request body:
  ```
    {
      "times": [
        "2025-06-10T00:00:00",
        "2025-06-10T01:00:00"
      ],
      "data": [
        "10", "12"
      ],
      "variable": "temperature",
      "units": "deg C"
    }
  ```
  Press execute
  Copy and paste output to file, e.g. plot.html

View HTML file
  Since the HTML file is sent as a JSON string, some editing needs doing so that you can open it in your browser, namely removing '"' from start and end of file, replacing the strings '\n' with actual new line chars and replacing '\"' with '"'.
  ```
    sed -i '1s/^"//g' plot.html 
    sed -i '$s/"$//g' plot.html 
    sed -i 's/\\n/\n/g' plot.html
    sed -i 's/\\"/"/g' plot.html
  ```
  There is a helper script that will do this for you:
  ```
    ./edit-html.sh plot.html
  ```
  now navigate to file:///{path to plot.html}
  and you should be able to see a plot.
